# JARVIS MK-1 — what git carries and what it must not.
#
# The rule: SOURCE and STRUCTURE are versioned. Secrets, biometrics and personal history are
# versioned too, but ONLY inside private/vault.enc (see tools/vault.sh) — encrypted before they ever
# reach a commit. Everything a machine can rebuild for itself is left out entirely.
#
# Why the vault instead of just committing the files: a private repo is not a vault. It is readable
# by anything holding an account token, it is in scope for an account breach, and git history is
# permanent — a key committed today and deleted tomorrow is still in the history forever. Encrypting
# first means a repo leak is not a data leak.

# ── secrets: real values live in the vault; .env.example documents the shape ──
.env
*.token
.apitoken
jarvis-os/state/.apitoken
jarvis-control/.token

# ── biometric + personal: family faces, life history, location. Vault only. ──
jarvis-os/faces/
jarvis-os/state/profile.json
jarvis-os/state/episodes.json
jarvis-os/state/memory.json
jarvis-os/state/location.json
jarvis-os/state/notifications.json

# ── logs. clap.log is a transcript of what the microphone heard in his home. ──
# Never committed, encrypted or otherwise: it is regenerable noise with a real privacy cost.
*.log
logs/
jarvis-os/logs/
clap-daemon/clap.log
focus-guard/focus.log
security/*.log

# ── regenerable: rebuilt by install.sh, so committing them only adds weight ──
jarvis-os/state/library.json
security/integrity.manifest
security/selfheal.json
security/canary.json
node_modules/

# ── per-run churn: rewritten by simply USING JARVIS, so the tree was dirty after
# every session and real changes had to be picked out of the noise. None of it is
# worth restoring onto a new machine — a canary re-arms, a greeting re-greets, the
# proactive clock restarts, the time machine refills from live use.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: lessons.json and learned.json. They churn too, but they
# hold what he taught and what failed — the one thing in state/ a dead Mac could
# not rebuild for itself.
jarvis-os/state/canary.json
jarvis-os/state/greeting-seen.json
jarvis-os/state/proactive.json
jarvis-os/state/timemachine.json
jarvis-os/state/listener.json
# Visual-silent mode's override. Runtime state with an expiry, exactly like listener.json above —
# the SCHEDULE is code (server.js) and belongs in git; a manual "quiet for 30 minutes" does not.
jarvis-os/state/silent.json
jarvis-os/state/spend.json
# The week's AI/ML stories (ainews.js): re-read from the sources by any dream cycle, three weeks at most.
jarvis-os/state/ainews.json
# The migration-version manifest. Machine-local and RESTORED FROM THE VAULT, not
# from git — it must arrive with the data files it describes (episodes, profile),
# which are vaulted too. A copy from git could name a version the restored data
# has not reached and re-run migrations over it.
jarvis-os/state/.schema.json

# ── build products: rebuilt from source by install.sh ──
*.app/
!**/Contents/
build/
*.o
# Python's own bytecode cache, rewritten whenever a .py changes (tts/piper_daemon.py's was committed once).
__pycache__/

# ── backups/: every pre-edit copy, and the leftovers of the 2026-09-18 tidy. git IS the history for
# tracked files; backups/ exists for the files git never sees (.env, jarvis.yaml, state/*.json) and for a
# one-command undo of an edit. tools/bak.sh FILE WHAT copies FILE to backups/<same path>.bak-WHAT-<time>.
# The patterns below still catch a .bak made the old way, beside its file — 346 of those had piled up.
backups/
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.bak.*

# ── macOS noise ──
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# ── live config: holds the settings-panel password hash. Vaulted, not committed. ──
jarvis.yaml

# Derived vector index for semantic recall — rebuilt from episodes.json in ~13s, 2.2MB of floats.
jarvis-os/state/episodes.vec
jarvis-os/state/episodes.vec.json

# The item classifier's cache. Unlike learned.json/lessons.json above, this is NOT a ledger of what
# he taught — it fills itself in, one row per item ever classified, so tracking it would put a diff
# on every order. The rows that ARE his (marked `taught`) are irreplaceable, so they go in the
# encrypted vault instead: tools/vault.sh carries this file. Everything else in it rebuilds by
# re-embedding, and the whole file is discarded automatically when the prototypes change.
jarvis-os/state/itemclass.json

# ── finetune/: the reader model's workshop. Scripts are versioned; everything heavy or personal is not.
# data/ holds his real sentences (same rule as state/norm_pairs.json). models/ and adapters/ are
# gigabytes a machine can re-download or re-train. .venv is rebuilt from requirements.txt.
finetune/.venv/
finetune/models/
finetune/data/
# adapters/current IS shipped (2026-09-24, owner's decision): the live reader's LoRA adapter, 29 MB, so any
# install can build jarvis-reader. It holds weights only — the messages it was trained on stay in data/.
finetune/adapters/*
!finetune/adapters/current/
finetune/runs/

# What JARVIS writes out for him (the Sunday AI/ML list, etc.) — personal output, not code.
JARVIS Outputs/

# Per-user speech vocabulary (names, places) — install.sh seeds it from config/vocabulary.example.json
config/vocabulary.json
